{{ElectionsSC}}
The '''1996 South Carolina United States Senate election''' was held on November 5, 1996, to select the [[United States Senate|U.S. Senator]] from the state of [[South Carolina]]. Incumbent [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] Senator [[Strom Thurmond]] won re-election against [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic]] challenger Elliott Springs Close for an eighth term in office. The margin, however, was one of the closest in Thurmond's 48-year Senate career. At the age of {{age in years, months and days|1902|12|02|1996|11|05}}, Thurmond became the oldest person ever to be re-elected to the United States Senate. He served out the entirety of his final term and left the Senate on January 3, 2003, at age 100 years and 29 days.

==Democratic primary==
The [[South Carolina Democratic Party]] held their [[Partisan primary|primary]] on June 11, 1996.  Elliott Springs Close, a 43-year-old political novice from [[Columbia, South Carolina|Columbia]], entered the Democratic primary and faced opposition from black photographer [[Cecil J. Williams]].  Close was a wealthy heir of a textile business, a brother-in-law of President [[Bill Clinton|Clinton's]] [[White House Chief of Staff|chief of staff]] [[Erskine Bowles]], who styled himself as a fiscal conservative and a social moderate.{{citation needed|date=October 2020}}

